# Where a loop writes only objects that were allocated in this function, what is known about every other object survives the loop header.
# Whether a value is another object is decided by the order of allocation: a value that existed before the first such object was allocated cannot be that object.
# Nothing is known about a value that came later (Walker::classifyAgainstAllocations()).
# Here the array that is read came later: it is the array allocated here, returned through veil(). So the guard before the loop tests that it is another array.
# Fault injection, testing=AssumeLaterValuesUnaliased: the compiler assumes that it is another object.
# The guard is hoisted, and the elements are read as numbers throughout the loop while each iteration pushes a string into the array.
# In a default build the results still look right, because an array into which a string literal is pushed is allocated with boxed storage and fails the hoisted guard.
# The wrong numbers show up under garbage collections, where the array is allocated differently. The program is
# test/aot/parallel/a-push-into-a-list-made-here-writes-that-list-alone/a-written-text-is-pushed-into-the-list-whose-veiled-self-is-read-as-numbers.cjs.
